  [1mRecommendation: single-user, browser-only, with storage behind a thin module.[0m

  Why: there is no server, no auth, no deploy target, and no task model. Choosing "multi-user" here doesn't add
  notifications to your app — it commits you to building an app first, and the notifications feature becomes maybe 15%
  of that work. Browser-only gets you something real and testable now, and one small [38;5;153mstorage[39m module keeps the door open
  without being speculative scaffolding.

  [1mBut that choice rewrites what "notification" means,[0m and this is the part I want you to actually decide rather than
  inherit from a tech pick:

  - [1mWith a server[0m, a notification is [3msocial[0m: "Dana reassigned this to you," "someone commented." The point is other
    people.
  - [1mWithout one[0m, a notification is [3mtemporal and self-directed[0m: "this is due in an hour," "this went overdue," "you
    haven't touched this in a week." The point is your own attention. Nobody else changes your tasks, so there's no one
    else to be notified about.

  Both are legitimate notification systems. They share almost no design — different triggers, different data model,
  different delivery. Picking the wrong one and discovering it mid-build is the expensive mistake here, which is why I'd
  rather ask than assume.

  Your original phrasing — "tasks they care about change" — reads social to me. But that may just be how the idea got
  worded.
